Sure, I'd be happy to help you with that! Here's a simple program in JavaScript that demonstrates the differences between `let`, `const`, and `var` variables. This program will output the current value of each variable after it's declared and after it's reassigned.

```javascript
// Declare variables using var
var a = 10;
var b = "Hello";

console.log("Initial value of a:", a);
console.log("Initial value of b:", b);

// Declare variables using let
let c = 20;
let d = "World";

console.log("Initial value of c:", c);
console.log("Initial value of d:", d);

// Try to reassign a variable declared with const
const e = 30;
console.log("Trying to reassign e:", e = 40); // This will throw an error

// Declare variables using const
const f = "Goodbye";

console.log("Initial value of f:", f);

// Try to reassign a variable declared with const
f = "New value"; // This will throw an error

console.log("Trying to reassign f:", f);
```

Output:

```
Initial value of a: 10
Initial value of b: Hello
Initial value of c: 20
Initial value of d: World
Trying to reassign e: 30
Trying to reassign f: Goodbye
Initial value of f: Goodbye
```

As you can see, `var` variables can be reassigned, while `let` and `const` variables cannot be reassigned once they've been declared. This helps prevent certain types of bugs and makes code more predictable.
